A||||20||beer_stud_21||Will I fit in a fiero?||||5||
Z||000000||beer_stud_21||01-21-2001||04:34 PM||||Hey guys,<P>I just found this forum a few days ago and have been having fun reading over some of this stuff.  I don't yet own a fiero but have my eye on a 85GT/auto that I can get for cheap.  I have yet to check out the interior of this 85GT so I wanted to ask you guys how much interior room there is inside one of these things.  Im about 6'6" so am curious to know if I will even fit in a fiero before I get my heart set on buying this one.  I know fieros are pretty small cars but is there a fair amount of leg/head room?  Also- the above mentioned 85GT has abou 77k miles and currently doesnt run.  The current owner says it ran when he parked it and that it just needs to have the distributor cleaned up for it to start right up. (its been parked for at least 1-2 yrs, maybe more) I've already looked up how to get the trouble codes out of the computer but if you guys had any other buying tips for me that would be way cool.  The car is a V6 GT/auto with brand new rear tires and calipers for $600.00 the clear coat is totally gone so it looks a little weathered, but thats ok with me.  Also I know the body is plastic/fiberglass but i'm in hawaii so if you guys could tell me where to check for rust that would be great to know also.  <P>Thanks alot,<P>Marlin||||reg||5
Z||000001||moonie||01-21-2001||05:14 PM||||my friend is 6'5" 240lbs & he fits in mine.<BR>the guy i bought my current car from was HUGE & he must have fit in it . <BR>i don't know if i'd look for rust. i've seen some ragged fiero's but can't ever remember looking underneath & seeing rust. <P>isn't the frame aluminum ??????<P>heck, 600 bucks..... go for it. <BR>even if you spend 1,000 to fix stuff you still have a cool a$$ car for 1,600.<P>welcome to the forum <P><p>[This message has been edited by moonie (edited 01-21-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||stimpy||01-21-2001||05:19 PM||||As far as fit goes, I am a couple of hairs over 6'6", and weigh in about 285. 34" inseam. The Fiero is indeed what I'd call a tight fit, but I do fit.  In fact it is a better fit then many "larger" cars.  The design of the dash gives me knee room on either side of the instrument pod.  My brother has a 97 Camaro. Very claustrophobic fit and my knees press on the dash. I've ridden in a Montero Sport, couldn't hold my head straight.<BR>If the car has the sunnroof, you get probably an extra 1/2" of headroom. Doesn't sound like a lot to the vertcally challenged, but I'm sure you can appreciate what that 1/2" can mean.<BR>As far as rust goes, I've heard that the trunk is a good indicator of how rust has affected the car. My car is a California car, so I haven't seen any rust. Good luck, sounds like a great buy.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Loud Pipes Save Lives!      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/stimpy_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||fropuff||01-21-2001||05:19 PM||||The place that commonly rusts out first is the trunk.  Pull back the trunk carpeting and inspect it real good.  I guess you would check the frame too.  It does rust; not aluminum.  Good luck!<P>------------------<BR>Fuzz busters are for those who are worried about being caught.||||reg||1
Z||000004||beer_stud_21||01-21-2001||05:50 PM||||Thanks guys.  I'm only about 160lb so it sounds like I'll fit good.  And yes it does have a sun roof.  This is awsome news because I was beginning to worry that I would get all ready for a fiero and then sit down in one and realize it was an impossible fit.  It sounds like fieros have a lot of interior room considering how small they are.  Do you guys have any other buying tips you could pass along?  <P>marlin||||reg||14
Z||000005||beer_stud_21||01-21-2001||05:57 PM||||Stimpy, that reminds me... Have you ever ridden in a RX7?  hehe.  A friend of mine has one and that thing is damn small...  My head was practically sideways and I could barely fold my legs up enough to get the door closed.. hehe.  Right now I'm driving an 89 T-bird which fits good, but I like the idea of a mid engine car with a rust proof body(not counting the frame of course) especially in hawaii.  There arn't a lot of fieros around here but even the worst looking fiero is a lot better looking than a lot of the cars driving around just for the fact that they dont suffer from body rust.<P>marlin||||reg||1
Z||000006||GeeDee86GT||01-21-2001||06:47 PM||||I'm only 5'10", but 235 lbs. Fit just fine. I can only dream that there may be a problem in Hawaii, but for rust..... <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/garby_d/rust.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/garby_d/rust.html</A>    Mostly a problem with road salt here.  Bags o'fun tho can't beat 'em.<BR>Gary<BR>86 SE builder<BR>86 SE driver (clutch gone yesterday)<BR>84 parts beast<p>[This message has been edited by GeeDee86GT (edited 01-21-2001).]||||reg||6
Z||000007||1FST2M6||01-21-2001||07:39 PM||||DUDE 6-6 and 160.. i'm 6-2 and 170 and i'm damn skinny! HEH  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> you'll fit JUST fine.. my Friend "Redneck John" (yes we call him that to his face) is 6-6 and he drives my car all the time. his head doens't stick out of the sunroof or nothing! <P><BR>welcome to the forum!<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig1fst.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||stimpy||01-21-2001||10:27 PM||||160?! Holy crap man, eat a sandwich or a small person or something! I weighed 160 once, in 6th grade!!<BR>Some peoples metabolisms, I tell ya there ain't no justice.  You're probably one of those guys that puts buffets out of business too.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by stimpy (edited 01-21-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||StuGood||01-21-2001||10:46 PM||||Think most of the complaints I read/hear  involve getting in/out of Fieros, rather than any lack of room or comfort. Once inside, you'll probably feel very comfy.  So when you go try out the car, do the ingress/egress a few times to get used to it, and to see if it's something you can live with.<P>Lucky for me, I'm kind of average- to smallish.  But it's still tough for me to get out of a Fiero.  Physically, it's no problem - I just don't want to get out  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">! <P>As far as inspecting a used Fiero, look for things like headlights that don't come up, smoky exhaust on startup, clutch action, etc.  Personally, I <B>always</B> find something wrong and whine about it, to get the price down.||||reg||6
Z||000010||fropuff||01-21-2001||10:52 PM||||I agree; Fieros are extremely roomy.  I'm only 5'10" and 125, but I can still stretch out all the say in the passenger side and have an extra 8" til the floor board.  The driver's side is a little more constricted, with the pedals and all, but it's still very comfortable.  The only problem you will most likely have is bending down to get into it.  Might have to sit on the ground and roll in like my grandpa.   <P>------------------<BR>Fuzz busters are for those who are worried about being caught.||||reg||1
Z||000011||RaeQwan||01-21-2001||10:56 PM||||I'll have no questions from ya'll about my size thank ya'll very much. I'm 6'2 and 130lbs(on a good day) and I fit wonderfully hehe.. my brother is a average size 6'8 guy.. he fits rather well.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <P>------------------<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/raeqwansig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000012||beer_stud_21||01-21-2001||11:37 PM||||Well, I'm about to turn 20 and have been a skinny bastard all my life.  You wouldn't believe how hard it is for me to gain weight.  The matabolism thing can be a mixed blessing.  Im just glad im on the skinny side of the extreme instead of the other way around.  I dont think I would have a chance of getting in and out of a fiero if I was 6'6" and 350+<P>Oh yea, and the GT im looking at doesnt seem to have a spoiler.  Is there any reason why it wouldn't?  I dont really care that it doesnt have it because Im not really attached to how it makes it look.  Im fine with it either way but from what I've read the wing just hurts MPG and the auto that this car has will hurt the gas milage as it is so I want to try to keep the milage as reasonable as possible with medium grade gas costing like 2.10 here every little bit helps.<P>thanks for the info guys.  This board rocks from what ive seen so far.<P>-marlin||||reg||6
Z||000013||RaeQwan||01-22-2001||01:50 AM||||factory option to leave the wing off if I remember correctly?||||reg||1
Z||000014||Standard||01-22-2001||04:57 AM||||Yep, the only model the wing was ever standard on was the Formula.  <P>Welcome to the forum!||||reg||
Z||000015||87GT||01-22-2001||05:36 PM||||$2.10 for midgrade?  geez.<BR>Everyone that rides in my fiero always comments how much space there is for seating.  Much more roomy than you would think.||||reg||1
Z||000016||Black88GT||01-22-2001||05:42 PM||||Standard, don't forget the Indy....<P>For what its worth, in the Gary Witzenburg Fiero book it mentions the president or VP or something of GM at the time was like 6'4" and when he went to check out how the project was going, he couldn't fit!  So they redesigned it for taller people.<P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/cartmanico.gif">             <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/kennyico.gif">           <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/stanico.gif">         <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/kyleico.gif">             <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/chefico.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000017||jerpro||01-23-2001||04:43 PM||||HMMM 6,3 and 215 pounds (all muscle  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">) I fit great in a fiero. <BR>I am doing 5 hour trips down to ohio twice every month  (20 hrs) and it is very comfortable.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Only try your best, and if at first you don't succeed... TRY HARDER.||||reg||1
Z||000018||Songman||01-23-2001||07:45 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by beer_stud_21:<BR><B>Well, I'm about to turn 20 and have been a skinny bastard all my life.  You wouldn't believe how hard it is for me to gain weight.  The matabolism thing can be a mixed blessing.  Im just glad im on the skinny side of the extreme instead of the other way around.  I dont think I would have a chance of getting in and out of a fiero if I was 6'6" and 350+</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Hey! Don't get too caught up in that metabolism thing! I was 6' and 95 lbs when I left Jr. High. I was 6'2" and 135 when I left High School. I was 6'2" and 175 when I left basic training.... Now at 36 I am almost 6'3" and 210lbs! It WILL catch up with you more than likely! People figured I would always be a bone!<P>Just enjoy the Fiero! You'll fit in there fine!<P><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000019||Old Lar||01-23-2001||08:10 PM||||The wing was a $279 option in 87 for my GT.  It was a lot car and the dealer had ordered a bunch of Fieros at that time all loaded in all colors.  The only difference was some had the 5 speed and some had the automatic.<P>I'm up there with GeeDee in size and I find the Fiero most comfortable to ride in.  Getting in is easy, you just fall in.  Getting out is the challenge!||||reg||1
Z||000020||jerpro||01-24-2001||06:36 PM||||what I have noticed is that the taller/bigger you are the sooner you get rips in "the usuall spot" on the drivers seat.<BR>||||reg||1
